.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt CJQ 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cjq
.Nd query JSON from the shell
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar flags
.Ar query
.Op Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads one JSON value from
.Ar file
or standard input, applies
.Ar query
and writes each result to standard output.
Results are pretty-printed unless
.Fl c
is given; colors are used when standard output is a terminal.
.Pp
The query language is a path expression rooted at
.Ql $ :
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ic $.a.b
object member access
.It Ic $.a[2]
array item, zero based
.It Ic $.a[-1]
item counted from the end
.It Ic $.a[1:3]
slice, end exclusive; either bound may be omitted
.It Ic $."two words"
quoted member name
.It Ic $name
variable defined with
.Fl -arg
or
.Fl -argjson
.It Ic $.a[*] No or Ic $.a[]
every item of an array or every value of an object
.It Ic $.a?
optional step: a miss prints
.Dv null
instead of nothing
.It Ic $..uid
every member named
.Ql uid
at any depth
.It Ic $.a, $.b
print both expressions, in order
.It Ic $.age > 21
comparison with
.Ic == , != , < , <= , >
or
.Ic >=
.It Ic $.ok and $.age > 21
boolean operators
.Ic and
and
.Ic or
(the former binds tighter)
.It Ic $.a | length()
pipe results into a builtin call
.El
.Pp
Builtins:
.Ql length() ,
.Ql keys() ,
.Ql keys_unsorted() ,
.Ql values() ,
.Ql type() ,
.Ql has("key") ,
.Ql select(cond) ,
.Ql map(expr) ,
.Ql add() ,
.Ql contains(x) ,
.Ql startswith(s) ,
.Ql endswith(s) ,
.Ql join(sep) ,
.Ql split(sep) ,
.Ql unique() ,
.Ql group_by(expr) ,
.Ql flatten() ,
.Ql to_entries() ,
.Ql from_entries() ,
.Ql reverse() ,
.Ql test(regex) ,
.Ql match(regex) ,
.Ql fromjson() ,
.Ql tojson() ,
.Ql sort(field?) ,
.Ql max(field?) ,
.Ql min(field?) ,
.Ql max_by(expr) ,
.Ql min_by(expr) ,
.Ql tostring() ,
.Ql tonumber() ,
.Ql ascii_downcase() ,
.Ql ascii_upcase() ,
.Ql ltrimstr(s) ,
.Ql rtrimstr(s) ,
.Ql range(n) ,
.Ql range(from; to) ,
.Ql range(from; to; step) ,
.Ql path(expr) ,
.Ql del(expr) ,
.Ql sum(field?) ,
.Ql first() ,
.Ql last() ,
.Ql floor() ,
.Ql ceil() ,
.Ql abs() ,
.Ql sqrt() ,
.Ql ftos() ,
.Ql not() .
.Pp
.Ql test()
uses POSIX extended regular expressions.
An invalid pattern produces no result.
.Ql match()
returns the first match as an object with
.Ql offset ,
.Ql length ,
.Ql string
and a
.Ql captures
array; offsets count UTF-8 code points.
An invalid pattern or a miss produces no result.
.Pp
Builtin arguments are separated by semicolons.
.Ql range()
emits numbers starting at
.Ar from
(default 0) while below
.Ar to ,
adding
.Ar step
(default 1).
A zero step or a direction that never reaches
.Ar to
emits nothing, and at most one million numbers are emitted.
.Ql path()
returns the location of each value selected by its argument as an array of
object keys and array indexes.
.Ql del()
returns a copy of the input with the selected paths removed.
.Pp
Values
.Dv null ,
.Dv false ,
.Dv 0 ,
.Dv \&"" ,
.Dv []
and
.Dv {}
are falsy.
.Pp
Comparisons are scalar:
.Ic ==
and
.Ic !=
are deep equality, while
.Ic < , <= , >
and
.Ic >=
order by type and then by value.
A comparison with a missing operand produces no result.
.Pp
.Ic and
and
.Ic or
combine truthiness values;
.Ic and
binds tighter than
.Ic or .
Both bind looser than comparisons and tighter than pipes.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl -compact
Print compact JSON, one line per result, without spaces.
.It Fl r , Fl -raw
Print string results without quotes.
.It Fl j , Fl -join-output
Do not write a newline after each result.
.It Fl a , Fl -ascii-output
Escape bytes outside ASCII as
.Ql \euXXXX ,
using surrogate pairs for code points above U+FFFF.
.It Fl s , Fl -slurp
Read every whitespace-separated JSON value and present them as one array.
.It Fl R , Fl -raw-input
Read raw text instead of JSON.
Without
.Fl s
each input line becomes a string and the query runs once per line; with
.Fl s
the whole input becomes one string.
A trailing carriage return is removed from each line.
.It Fl e , Fl -exit-status
Exit 1 when the result is falsy or empty, 0 otherwise.
.It Fl n , Fl -null-input
Run the query against
.Dv null
and do not read input.
.It Fl C , Fl -color
Colorize the output even when stdout is not a terminal.
.It Fl M , Fl -no-color , Fl -monochrome
Never colorize.  The environment variable
.Ev CJQ_NO_COLOR
has the same effect.
.It Fl -indent Ar n
Indent pretty output with
.Ar n
spaces, where
.Ar n
is between 0 and 8.
.It Fl -tab
Indent pretty output with one tab.
.It Fl S , Fl -sort-keys
Sort object members by key in the output.
.It Fl f , Fl -from-file Ar file
Read the query from
.Ar file
instead of the query argument; the remaining positional argument is the
input file.
.It Fl -arg Ar name value
Define the variable
.Ql $name
as the string
.Ar value .
.It Fl -argjson Ar name json
Define the variable
.Ql $name
as the JSON value parsed from
.Ar json .
An invalid or incomplete JSON value is a usage error.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage information.
.It Fl v , Fl -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CJQ_NO_COLOR
.It Ev CJQ_NO_COLOR
Any value disables colored output.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Success (truthy result under
.Fl e ) .
.It 1
Falsy or empty result while
.Fl e
is active.
.It 2
Usage error (unknown flag, missing query or bad option value).
.It 3
The input is not valid JSON, or contains trailing data.
.It 4
The query does not parse, or names an unknown builtin or wrong arity.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Extract one field:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo '{"status":"ok"}' | cjq \&'$.status'
"ok"
.Ed
.Pp
Print raw strings, one per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cjq -r \&'$.users[*].name' users.json
.Ed
.Pp
Use as a script test:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if echo "$response" | cjq -e \&'$.ok'; then
	echo healthy
fi
.Ed
.Pp
Summarize a list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s https://api.example.dev/orders | cjq -r \e
	\&'$.data | sum(.total)'
.Ed
.Pp
Filter and transform:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cjq -r \&'$.users | select(.age > 21) | map(.name)' users.json
.Ed
.Pp
Pass a shell value into a query:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cjq -n --arg name Ada \&'$name'
"Ada"
.Ed
.Pp
Convert a numeric string to a number:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo '" 42 "' | cjq -c \&'$. | tonumber()'
42
.Ed
.Pp
Read lines of text instead of JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf 'alpha\enbeta\en' | cjq -R -r .
.Ed
